 Here is a sample Semgrep rule in YAML format for the vulnerability-fixing evidence you provided:

```yaml
id: "vuln-openslides-auth-service-70c1aa9f"
title: "Potential Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) in UserService.readUserFromDatastoreByCredentials()"
description: |
  This rule detects potential IDOR vulnerabilities in the `readUserFromDatastoreByCredentials` method of the `UserService` class.
  The vulnerability is present because the function does not check if the user object exists before attempting to access its properties or methods, which could lead to a null pointer exception.
severity: WARNING
languages:
  - typescript
metadata:
  tags: ['security', 'idor']
pattern: |
  # This is a comment line
  ^function readUserFromDatastoreByCredentials\(username, password\) {
    const userObj = await this.getUserCollectionFromDatastore\('username'\);
    Logger.debug\('User object from datastore: ', userObj\);
    const users = Object.values\(userObj\)\.filter\(user => !user.meta_deleted\);
    if \(users.length > 1\) {
      Logger.error\('Multiple users found for same username!\'\)
      throw new AuthenticationException\('Multiple users with same credentials!\'\);
    }
    const thisUser: User = new User\(users[0]\);
    const passwordCorrect = await this.isPasswordCorrect\(password, thisUser\.password || dummyPassword\);
    if \(!thisUser.isExisting\(\) || !passwordCorrect\) {
      throw new AuthenticationException\('Username or password is incorrect.\'\);
    }
    if \(!thisUser.is_active\) {
      throw new AuthenticationException\('The account is deactivated.\'\);
    }
    // migrate old passwords
    if \(this._hashingHandler.isDeprecatedHash\(thisUser.password\)\) {
      const newHash = await this._hashingHandler.hash\(password\);
      await this.updateUser\(thisUser.id, \{ password: newHash \}\);
    }
    return thisUser;
  }$
```

This rule will match the vulnerable behavior of not checking if a user object exists before accessing its properties or methods, which could lead to a null pointer exception. The pattern is written in JavaScript RegExp syntax and should be compatible with Semgrep's YAML rules.

